ure of his compositions."},{"id":"bad4e910-7155-47c6-a3f5-c39c9708e4f9","text":"He freed music from hitherto prevailing conventions of harmony and structure."},{"id":"0a0cc1ed-92cc-4ee1-ae98-251df5de8c7a","text":"Sometimes I feel in his late works a will to break all signs of continuity."},{"id":"2e6c0059-f8ba-44b9-a733-a49b51790453","text":"The music is abrupt and seemingly disconnected, as in the last piano sonata."},{"id":"bb8aaf7f-03c1-44e3-a0c2-bed3512b141d","text":"In musical expression, he did not feel restrained by the weight of convention."},{"id":"f4ba7280-f32b-4608-ac42-0ced2be0ce4c","text":"By all accounts he was a freethinking person, and a courageous one, and I find courage an essential quality for the understanding, let alone the performance, of his works."}]},{"id":"ba1d0d9f-a35f-4508-aaa3-33571a900559","sentences":[{"id":"e9f84473-d3ab-46d2-a234-c0c9ba001f35","text":"This courageous attitude in fact becomes a requirement for the performers of Beethoven’s music."},{"id":"09142651-5552-4445-a426-b9ae25e05e73","text":"His compositions demand the performer to show courage, for example in the use of dynamics."},{"id":"0f42d9f2-19c6-4d4d-afcd-60c177cec7a8","text":"Beethoven’s habit of increasing the volume with an extreme intensity and then abruptly following it with a sudden soft passage was only rarely used by composers before him."}]},{"id":"cfa31751-4e9a-4785-aa8f-bf0a69738f14","sentences":[{"id":"5071e486-ab49-46e3-a240-3826256142f9","text":"Beethoven was a deeply political man in the broadest sense of the word."},{"id":"8424e564-83da-4021-afce-10cd12376024","text":"He was not interested in daily politics, but concerned with questions of moral behavior and the larger questions of right and wrong affecting the entire society."}]},{"id":"e2f2f57a-91be-4a3e-a09b-b6aecf127b13","sentences":[{"id":"729d4ac1-4d74-49ae-ae24-83a1363b12fe","text":"Especially significant was his view of freedom, which, for him, was associated with the rights and responsibilities of the individual: he advocated freedom of thought and of personal expression."}]},{"id":"9896f080-5db4-445c-ae5e-ec6e1643bac0","sentences":[{"id":"e907774a-1f58-4822-a52f-b97736f9b5d5","text":"Beethoven’s music tends to move from chaos to order as if order were an imperative of human existence."},{"id":"20ca2194-de1a-4bdd-ada3-564a881e2c53","text":"For him, order does not result from forgetting or ignoring the disorders that plague our existence; order is a necessary development, an improvement that may lead to the Greek ideal of spiritual elevation."},{"id":"cf3cec0a-82b8-43c2-a859-63b270f41f66","text":"It is not by chance that the Funeral March is not the last movement of the Eroica Symphony, but the second, so that suffering does not have the last word."},{"id":"57f79179-ac70-4fc4-a39b-7d7a9cf95282","text":"One could interpret much of the work of Beethoven by saying that suffering is inevitable, but the courage to fight it renders life worth living."}]}],"sentences":[{"id":"07de8b0d-d0a7-42d6-adbf-6e6b36161b78","text":"Music means different things to different people and sometimes even different things to the same person at different moments of his life."},{"id":"512c50d7-f007-44b5-a88a-ba5e46c78d19","text":"It might be poetic, philosophical, sensual, or mathematical, but in any case it must, in my view, have something to do with the soul of the human being."},{"id":"61ed4e3e-472d-40e7-ad15-1cb5458d261d","text":"Hence it is metaphysical; but the means of expression is purely and exclusively physical: sound."},{"id":"823e8bf6-5fd0-4fdf-abf1-cfd3039982cb","text":"I believe it is precisely this permanent coexistence of metaphysical message through physical means that is the strength of music."},{"id":"80411be6-004c-4184-a5f8-aed9f4fc4019","text":"It is also the reason why when we try to describe music with words, all we can do is articulate our reactions to it, and not grasp music itself."},{"id":"911e47df-2b64-44ef-ac3c-7afe73022dbb","text":"Beethoven’s importance in music has been principally defined by the revolutionary nature of his compositions."},{"id":"bad4e910-7155-47c6-a3f5-c39c9708e4f9","text":"He freed music from hitherto prevailing conventions of harmony and structure."},{"id":"0a0cc1ed-92cc-4ee1-ae98-251df5de8c7a","text":"Sometimes I feel in his late works a will to break all signs of continuity."},{"id":"2e6c0059-f8ba-44b9-a733-a49b51790453","text":"The music is abrupt and seemingly disconnected, as in the last piano sonata."},{"id":"bb8aaf7f-03c1-44e3-a0c2-bed3512b141d","text":"In musical expression, he did not feel restrained by the weight of convention."},{"id":"f4ba7280-f32b-4608-ac42-0ced2be0ce4c","text":"By all accounts he was a freethinking person, and a courageous one, and I find courage an essential quality for the understanding, let alone the performance, of his works."},{"id":"e9f84473-d3ab-46d2-a234-c0c9ba001f35","text":"This courageous attitude in fact becomes a requirement for the performers of Beethoven’s music."},{"id":"09142651-5552-4445-a426-b9ae25e05e73","text":"His compositions demand the performer to show courage, for example in the use of dynamics."},{"id":"0f42d9f2-19c6-4d4d-afcd-60c177cec7a8","text":"Beethoven’s habit of increasing the volume with an extreme intensity and then abruptly following it with a sudden soft passage was only rarely used by composers before him."},{"id":"5071e486-ab49-46e3-a240-3826256142f9","text":"Beethoven was a deeply political man in the broadest sense of the word."},{"id":"8424e564-83da-4021-afce-10cd12376024","text":"He was not interested in daily politics, but concerned with questions of moral behavior and the larger questions of right and wrong affecting the entire society."},{"id":"729d4ac1-4d74-49ae-ae24-83a1363b12fe","text":"Especially significant was his view of freedom, which, for him, was associated with the rights and responsibilities of the individual: he advocated freedom of thought and of personal expression."},{"id":"e907774a-1f58-4822-a52f-b97736f9b5d5","text":"Beethoven’s music tends to move from chaos to order as if order were an imperative of human existence."},{"id":"20ca2194-de1a-4bdd-ada3-564a881e2c53","text":"For him, order does not result from forgetting or ignoring the disorders that plague our existence; order is a necessary development, an improvement that may lead to the Greek ideal of spiritual elevation."},{"id":"cf3cec0a-82b8-43c2-a859-63b270f41f66","text":"It is not by chance that the Funeral March is not the last movement of the Eroica Symphony, but the second, so that suffering does not have the last word."},{"id":"57f79179-ac70-4fc4-a39b-7d7a9cf95282","text":"One could interpret much of the work of Beethoven by saying that suffering is inevitable, but the courage to fight it renders life worth living."}],"currentSentenceIndex":0,"origin":"system"}'::jsonb,
